1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,631
Take the five letter tiles of APPLE.

2
00:00:02,631 --> 00:00:06,588
Shuffle them to make new words,
real or not, like PLAPE or EPLAP.

3
00:00:06,988 --> 00:00:10,427
Five different letters make
one hundred and twenty words.

4
00:00:10,427 --> 00:00:12,222
But APPLE makes only sixty.

5
00:00:12,672 --> 00:00:15,762
Aarav found this while playing
a word game after class.

6
00:00:16,112 --> 00:00:17,229
Same five tiles.

7
00:00:17,229 --> 00:00:19,434
So where did half of the words go?

8
00:00:19,934 --> 00:00:21,703
The answer is the two P's.

9
00:00:21,703 --> 00:00:24,784
Swap them, and the word
looks exactly the same.

10
00:00:25,434 --> 00:00:27,864
Let us see this on a smaller word first.

11
00:00:28,214 --> 00:00:30,260
Take the letters A, P and P.

12
00:00:30,260 --> 00:00:34,542
Paint one P orange and the other
purple, so we can tell them apart.

13
00:00:34,942 --> 00:00:37,105
Now we have three different tiles.

14
00:00:37,105 --> 00:00:39,896
They can be arranged in
three factorial ways.

15
00:00:39,896 --> 00:00:40,860
That is six.

16
00:00:41,260 --> 00:00:42,632
Here they are, all six.

17
00:00:43,032 --> 00:00:44,744
Now wash off the paint.

18
00:00:44,744 --> 00:00:46,769
Both P's look the same again.

19
00:00:47,169 --> 00:00:48,392
Watch the pairs.

20
00:00:48,392 --> 00:00:52,083
Orange first or purple first,
it is now the same word.

21
00:00:52,083 --> 00:00:53,874
Each pair melts into one.

22
00:00:54,524 --> 00:00:55,821
Six becomes three.

23
00:00:55,821 --> 00:00:58,013
APP, PAP, and PPA.

24
00:00:58,663 --> 00:00:59,667
Side by side.

25
00:00:59,667 --> 00:01:02,531
With paint, the two P's
are different objects.

26
00:01:02,531 --> 00:01:04,864
Swapping them makes a new arrangement.

27
00:01:05,264 --> 00:01:07,623
Without paint, they are the same object.

28
00:01:07,623 --> 00:01:09,318
Swapping them changes nothing.

29
00:01:09,918 --> 00:01:11,024
Now you try.

30
00:01:11,024 --> 00:01:13,181
In APPLE, swap the two P's.

31
00:01:13,181 --> 00:01:16,014
Is it a new word, or the same word?

32
00:01:16,014 --> 00:01:17,388
Pause and decide.

33
00:01:20,588 --> 00:01:21,597
The same word!

34
00:01:21,597 --> 00:01:23,758
Nobody can tell which P went where.

35
00:01:23,758 --> 00:01:25,695
So we must not count it twice.

36
00:01:26,345 --> 00:01:27,855
Now let us find the rule.

37
00:01:28,205 --> 00:01:30,779
Pretend all five tiles
of APPLE are different.

38
00:01:30,779 --> 00:01:33,122
Then there are five
factorial arrangements.

39
00:01:33,122 --> 00:01:34,957
That is one hundred and twenty.

40
00:01:35,707 --> 00:01:39,051
But every real word was
counted more than once.

41
00:01:39,051 --> 00:01:43,173
Its two P's can swap in two
factorial ways, which is two.

42
00:01:43,923 --> 00:01:46,740
So each real word was counted two times.

43
00:01:46,740 --> 00:01:48,701
We divide by two factorial.

44
00:01:49,451 --> 00:01:52,244
One hundred and twenty over two is sixty.

45
00:01:52,994 --> 00:01:56,354
In general: n objects, where
p of them are the same.

46
00:01:56,354 --> 00:01:59,796
The number of arrangements is
n factorial over p factorial.

47
00:02:00,546 --> 00:02:05,088
If more groups repeat, divide
by the factorial of each group.

48
00:02:05,088 --> 00:02:10,044
n factorial over p one factorial
times p two factorial, and so on.

49
00:02:10,794 --> 00:02:12,401
Watch the count shrink.

50
00:02:12,401 --> 00:02:14,605
Start with five different letters.

51
00:02:14,605 --> 00:02:16,564
One hundred and twenty words.

52
00:02:16,964 --> 00:02:18,829
Make two letters the same.

53
00:02:18,829 --> 00:02:19,729
Sixty.

54
00:02:19,444 --> 00:02:20,947
Make three the same.

55
00:02:20,947 --> 00:02:21,847
Twenty.

56
00:02:22,012 --> 00:02:23,171
Four the same.

57
00:02:23,171 --> 00:02:24,071
Just five.

58
00:02:24,064 --> 00:02:25,490
All five the same.

59
00:02:25,490 --> 00:02:26,390
Only one.

60
00:02:26,931 --> 00:02:27,831
Quick one.

61
00:02:27,685 --> 00:02:28,595
AAAAA.

62
00:02:28,595 --> 00:02:30,103
How many different words?

63
00:02:30,103 --> 00:02:31,170
Pause and think.

64
00:02:34,370 --> 00:02:35,270
Just one!

65
00:02:35,234 --> 00:02:37,513
Every swap gives the same word.

66
00:02:37,513 --> 00:02:40,435
Five factorial over five factorial is one.

67
00:02:41,085 --> 00:02:42,102
Remember this.

68
00:02:42,102 --> 00:02:45,858
When letters repeat, divide by
the factorial of each repeat.

69
00:02:46,558 --> 00:02:48,248
Now, one worked example.

70
00:02:48,598 --> 00:02:52,456
How many different words can be
made from all the letters of BANANA?

71
00:02:52,956 --> 00:02:53,856
Step one.

72
00:02:53,766 --> 00:02:55,080
Count the letters.

73
00:02:55,080 --> 00:02:56,047
Six in all.

74
00:02:56,047 --> 00:02:58,141
Three A's, two N's and one B.

75
00:02:58,891 --> 00:02:59,791
Step two.

76
00:02:59,657 --> 00:03:03,936
If all six were different, there
would be six factorial, which is seven

77
00:03:03,936 --> 00:03:05,225
hundred and twenty.

78
00:03:05,975 --> 00:03:06,884
Step three.

79
00:03:06,884 --> 00:03:11,312
Divide by three factorial for the
A's, and two factorial for the N's.

80
00:03:12,062 --> 00:03:12,962
Step four.

81
00:03:12,903 --> 00:03:15,734
Seven hundred and twenty
over six times two.

82
00:03:15,734 --> 00:03:18,614
That is seven hundred
and twenty over twelve.

83
00:03:19,364 --> 00:03:21,351
So there are sixty different words.

84
00:03:22,001 --> 00:03:23,462
Now, the exam trap.

85
00:03:23,812 --> 00:03:26,028
Many students add the repeats first.

86
00:03:26,028 --> 00:03:29,568
They write six factorial over
five factorial, and get six.

87
00:03:29,968 --> 00:03:32,295
But each group swaps on its own.

88
00:03:32,295 --> 00:03:35,812
So we multiply: three
factorial times two factorial.

89
00:03:35,812 --> 00:03:37,339
The answer is sixty.

90
00:03:37,989 --> 00:03:39,123
Where is this asked?

91
00:03:39,123 --> 00:03:42,690
In JEE Main, as questions on arranging
the letters of a word with repeated

92
00:03:42,690 --> 00:03:43,590
letters.

93
00:03:43,831 --> 00:03:44,879
Exam level now.

94
00:03:44,879 --> 00:03:49,266
In how many arrangements of the letters
of SUCCESS are all three S's together?

95
00:03:49,266 --> 00:03:50,359
Pause and solve.

96
00:03:55,759 --> 00:03:57,305
The answer is A.

97
00:03:57,305 --> 00:03:58,205
Sixty.

98
00:03:58,536 --> 00:04:00,955
Glue the three S's into one block.

99
00:04:00,955 --> 00:04:02,937
Now we arrange five things.

100
00:04:03,437 --> 00:04:05,527
The block, U, C, C and E.

101
00:04:05,527 --> 00:04:06,836
The two C's repeat.

102
00:04:07,336 --> 00:04:10,831
So the count is five
factorial over two factorial.

103
00:04:10,831 --> 00:04:13,784
One hundred and twenty over two is sixty.

104
00:04:14,384 --> 00:04:15,924
Here is your recap.

105
00:04:15,924 --> 00:04:17,507
Screenshot this card.

106
00:04:20,707 --> 00:04:22,893
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

107
00:04:23,293 --> 00:04:24,193
One.

108
00:04:23,799 --> 00:04:27,781
Why does APPLE give sixty words,
not one hundred and twenty?

109
00:04:28,681 --> 00:04:29,581
Two.

110
00:04:29,078 --> 00:04:31,810
How many words can be made
from the letters of TOOT?

111
00:04:32,710 --> 00:04:33,610
Three.

112
00:04:33,279 --> 00:04:36,360
Why do we divide by three
factorial for three A's?

113
00:04:37,460 --> 00:04:39,715
Next time: pick two friends for a team.

114
00:04:39,715 --> 00:04:41,296
Does the order still matter?

115
00:04:41,296 --> 00:04:42,196
Not anymore.

116
00:04:42,083 --> 00:04:43,113
Meet combinations.
